{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-339627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+({x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+1=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =1+3\\cdot (-1)+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈0.00.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8399374ca9d7fa23e144fa51212e5981298d9a48bdbb69a8788fd40a7eb12a4"} {"question" "q-339627" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339627" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.00"] ["math" "1.03"] ["math" "2.50"] ["math" "3.21"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339627" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.00"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5x+3y,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+3y+(5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+3{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+3{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y+3{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+2=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(5\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈2.00.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8399374ca9d7fa23e144fa51212e5981298d9a48bdbb69a8788fd40a7eb12a4"} {"question" "q-339749" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339749" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2.00"] ["math" "2.13"] ["math" "2.21"] ["math" "2.50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339749" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.00"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2y+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.00.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8399374ca9d7fa23e144fa51212e5981298d9a48bdbb69a8788fd40a7eb12a4"} {"question" "q-339756" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339756" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.00"] ["math" "1.03"] ["math" "1.32"] ["math" "1.50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339756" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.00"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-3y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7} \\\\  & ≈1.14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈1.00,\\,y(2)≈1.14.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8399374ca9d7fa23e144fa51212e5981298d9a48bdbb69a8788fd40a7eb12a4"} {"question" "q-339772" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339772" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1.03"] ["math" "1.00"] ["math" "1.50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339772" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.00"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339772" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339772" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1.50"] ["math" "1.00"] ["math" "1.14"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339772" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=25x+5y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})$ and $y\\,(\\frac{2}{5}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈{{field-1}},\\,y\\,(\\frac{2}{5})≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{2}{5}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ is $\\frac{2}{5},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\frac{2}{5}-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((25x+5y)+(25{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{5})$ and $y(\\frac{2}{5}).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{5},\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{5})≈1.00,\\,y(\\frac{2}{5})≈6.00.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8399374ca9d7fa23e144fa51212e5981298d9a48bdbb69a8788fd40a7eb12a4"} {"question" "q-339871" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339871" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.25"] ["math" "1.00"] ["math" "2.00"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060680/state.json" "file_sha256" "a571cf0b01c1bf4f35bfc1edf47bb6d98a8e0c43c9287b7f1a5787970a088988"} {"question" "q-339871" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.00"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-work